#!/usr/bin/python3
"""Write one ephemeral session's desktop background, so that two desktops on
one machine do not look pixel-for-pixel identical.

    hdw4s-background write <instance> <output.svg> [--incarnation <file>]
    hdw4s-background check [--anchors L:h,L:h,...]

WHY THIS EXISTS. Every ephemeral session reads the same machine-wide dconf
database, so the perceptual distance between any two live desktops is exactly
zero: the only thing on screen that differs is the browser's address bar, and
the cheap screenshot reader resolved one address bar in four. A visitor with
two desktops open cannot tell which tab is which, and neither can anybody
reading a screenshot afterwards.

WHAT IT PRODUCES. Perlin noise over one of three brand anchors, varying HUE as
well as lightness. Two independently seeded turbulence fields, and that is the
point rather than an embellishment: a single field makes hue and lightness move
together, so a viewer who loses one channel loses both at once. Measured with
two fields, a dichromat keeps roughly 70% of the per-session distance.

The anchors, the band and the field strength are NOT tunable here and are not
guesses. Three anchors, band L* 29-45, k = 1.5, approved 2026-09-24 against a
rendered sheet; the measurements behind each number are in
private/DESIGN-per-session-background.md sections 4.9 and 4.10. The band is
bounded below by taste and above by contrast: white icon labels sit on this
field, and at the lightest pixel of band E they read 8.2:1, above WCAG AAA.

NO NEW PACKAGE DEPENDENCY, and that is why this emits SVG text rather than
pixels. GNOME rasterises the wallpaper itself through gdk-pixbuf's SVG loader,
which is the loader it already uses for every other image; nothing here has to
render, so nothing here needs a rendering library. python3 is already a
dependency of this package.

HOW THE HUE WALK IS BUILT, because SVG filters cannot rotate hue per pixel:
feColorMatrix's hueRotate takes ONE angle for a whole layer. So two layers are
composited at hue h-swing and h+swing, the upper masked by its own turbulence
field -- where the noise is high the pixel reads as the warmer hue, where it is
low as the cooler one. A second, independently seeded field at a different base
frequency drives lightness on top of that.

THE VIEWBOX IS 480x270 AND THE IMAGE IS NOT. feTurbulence's baseFrequency is
per USER UNIT, so the pattern's feature size follows the viewBox and not the
pixel size. Every measurement behind the approved parameters was taken on a
480-unit-wide field; emitting width=1920 with a 1920-unit viewBox would quarter
the feature size and render a design nobody has looked at. The nominal pixel
size is large so that the loader rasterises something worth scaling.
"""

